course: space
lesson: 8
title: Mars
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Mars, the fourth planet, further from the Sun than the Earth, is the red planet, and it is red for the same reason an old bike goes orange: its dust and rocks are full of iron oxide, rust.
explain: It is about half the Earth's width, and its day is almost like ours, only 40 minutes longer, but its thin air is mostly carbon dioxide and it is far colder than Antarctica on average.
explain: Mars has record-breakers: Olympus Mons, a volcano two and a half times taller than Everest, and Valles Marineris, a canyon that would stretch across Europe.
explain: Long ago water flowed there in rivers and lakes; today it is frozen in ice caps and underground, and rovers like Curiosity and Perseverance search the old lake beds for signs of ancient life.
explain: No life has been found yet; a 2025 rock sample has patterns that could be a sign of old microbes, but scientists say it is only a possible sign, not proof.
fact: Mars looks red because its surface dust and rocks contain iron oxide, which is rust.
fact: Mars is about half the Earth's width, and its day, called a sol, is about 24 hours 40 minutes.
fact: Mars has a very thin carbon dioxide atmosphere, less than one percent of the Earth's, and an average temperature of about minus 60 degrees Celsius.
fact: Olympus Mons on Mars is the tallest volcano in the Solar System, about 22 kilometres high, about two and a half times the height of Everest above sea level.
fact: Valles Marineris is a canyon system about 4,000 kilometres long and up to 7 kilometres deep, which would stretch across the whole of Europe.
fact: Mars once had rivers, lakes and perhaps a sea, and today its water is mostly frozen in polar ice caps and under the ground.
fact: NASA's Curiosity rover has been exploring Gale Crater since 2012, and Perseverance has explored Jezero Crater, an old lake bed, since 2021.
fact: No life has been found on Mars, but in 2025 scientists reported that a Perseverance rock sample has chemical patterns that could be a possible sign of ancient microbes; this is not proof.
fact: Curiosity found smooth, rounded pebbles like those in a stream, a sign that water once flowed there.
fact: Mars has dust storms that can cover the whole planet for weeks; a storm in 2018 blocked the sunlight and covered the solar panels of the rover Opportunity, and it never woke again.
fact: Mars has two small, lumpy moons, Phobos and Deimos, probably captured asteroids or pieces from an ancient impact; scientists are not yet sure which.
fact: Phobos is spiralling slowly inwards, and in about 50 million years it will crash into Mars or break into a ring.
exercise: Why does Mars look red? | Its dust and rocks contain iron oxide, rust | It is very hot, so it glows like metal in a fire | It is covered in red plants | Its sky reflects the red light of the Sun
exercise: How wide is Mars compared with the Earth? | About half the Earth's width | About the same width | About twice the Earth's width | About a tenth of the Earth's width
exercise: What is a day on Mars called, and how long is it? | A sol, about 24 hours 40 minutes | A sol, about 10 hours | A lunar day, about 27 days | A Mars-day, about 243 Earth days
exercise: What is Mars's atmosphere like? | Very thin and mostly carbon dioxide | Thick and warm, and full of fresh oxygen | Just like the Earth's, only a bit colder | Made of thick yellow sulphuric acid clouds
exercise: What is the average temperature on Mars? | About minus 60 degrees Celsius | About 20 degrees Celsius | About 465 degrees Celsius | About minus 224 degrees Celsius
exercise: What is Olympus Mons? | The tallest volcano in the Solar System | The deepest canyon anywhere on the planet Mars | The bigger of the two small moons of Mars | A big crater where a rover landed in 2012
exercise: How tall is Olympus Mons? | About 22 kilometres | About 8.8 kilometres | About 220 kilometres | About 2 kilometres
exercise: What is Valles Marineris? | A canyon system about 4,000 kilometres long | A dried-up river 40 kilometres long | A mountain range at the south pole, under the ice | The landing site of Perseverance
exercise: Where is most of Mars's water today? | Frozen in polar ice caps and under the ground | In big blue lakes and rivers right on the surface | In clouds of rain | Mars has never had water
exercise: What did Mars have long ago? | Rivers, lakes and perhaps a sea | Thick forests and green grasslands | Deep oceans of cold liquid methane | Bright, icy rings just like Saturn's
exercise: Which rover has explored Gale Crater since 2012? | Curiosity | Perseverance | Sojourner | Lunokhod
exercise: What is Jezero Crater? | An old lake bed explored by Perseverance | The tallest volcano on Mars | A crater on the Moon | The place where Phobos will crash into Mars one day
exercise: Has life been found on Mars? | No, but a 2025 sample shows a possible sign of ancient microbes | Yes, rovers have photographed animals | Yes, plants grow near the ice caps | No, and scientists have stopped looking
exercise: How big can dust storms on Mars get? | They can cover the whole planet for weeks | They are never more than a few metres wide | They last only a few seconds | Mars has no wind, so no storms
exercise: What are the names of Mars's two moons? | Phobos and Deimos | Io and Europa | Titan and Enceladus | Luna and Selene
exercise: What will probably happen to Phobos in about 50 million years? | It will crash into Mars or break into a ring | It will fly away into space and leave Mars behind | It will slowly grow bigger until it is a planet | It will join together with Deimos to make one moon
test: lol is Mars red cos it's super hot like lava? | No, it is cold; the red is rust in its dust and rocks | Yes, it glows red from the heat of its lava, like a fire | Yes, it is covered in lava seas | No, it is painted by the Sun
test: In a film, an astronaut on Mars takes off her helmet and breathes. What is wrong? | The air is very thin carbon dioxide, so she could not breathe | Nothing, Mars has air like ours | The air is fine but too hot | She would float away
test: Ella says Everest is the tallest mountain in the Solar System. What is the truth? | Olympus Mons on Mars is about two and a half times taller | She is right, Everest is the tallest anywhere | The Moon's mountains are the tallest | Jupiter has the tallest mountain
test: A Mars rover finds smooth, rounded pebbles like those in a stream. What do they suggest? | Water once flowed there long ago | Someone polished them recently | They fell from the Moon | Wind on Mars rolls them round every day
test: If you lived on Mars, would your day feel much longer than on Earth? | Only a little, a sol is about 40 minutes longer | Yes, a single day there lasts for a whole Earth month | Yes, the Sun never sets | No, a day lasts only 10 hours
test: news says "NASA FOUND LIFE ON MARS!!!" in 2025. What did scientists actually report? | A rock with chemical patterns that could be a possible sign of ancient microbes | Living animals under the ice | Bones of old Martians | Green plants in a crater
test: Why did the Mars rovers go to old crater lake beds? | Old lake beds are good places to look for signs of past life | Craters are the warmest places on Mars | It is the only flat ground on Mars | Craters have the best phone signal
test: Why might a huge dust storm be a problem for a solar-powered rover? | Dust blocks sunlight and covers its solar panels | Dust makes the rover heavier than a car, so it gets stuck | The storm blows the rover to the Moon | Dust storms on Mars are made of acid
test: A game shows Mars with one big moon like ours. What should it show? | Two small, lumpy moons, Phobos and Deimos | No moons at all, only a thin ring of red dust | Seventy moons, just like the giant planets have | Three moons and a bright ring, like a tiny Saturn
test: Valles Marineris placed on a map of Europe would reach... | From one side of Europe to the other | Just across one small town and its fields | Across Britain only, from coast to coast | Just round a football pitch and the stands
test: Why is Mars much colder than the Earth? | It is further from the Sun and its thin air holds little heat | It spins backwards | It has no Sun shining on it | Its ice caps freeze the whole planet daily
exam: Identify the reason for which the surface of Mars presents a red appearance. | The presence of iron oxide in its surface materials | The incandescence of its surface owing to intense heat | The reflection of light from its moons | The presence of red vegetation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present state of water upon Mars? | It is principally frozen in the polar caps and beneath the surface | It is present in extensive surface oceans | It has at no time been present upon Mars | It exists solely as rainfall in the atmosphere
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the search for life upon Mars. | No life has been found, though a possible sign of ancient microbes was reported in 2025 | Living organisms were conclusively identified in 2012 | The search was abandoned upon the arrival of Curiosity | Fossilised animals were recovered by Perseverance
exam: Ascertain the distinction held by Olympus Mons. | It is the tallest volcano in the Solar System | It is the deepest canyon in the whole Solar System | It is the largest moon of Mars | It is the largest impact crater upon Mars
